{
  "title": "05 — Learn How to Change Bolovan",
  "description": "You now understand the running system. End the story from a maintainer's seat: decide what must remain true, find the module that owns a policy, use accepted decisions and behavioral tests as guardrails, prove the production bundle, and keep this walkthrough synchronized with reality.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## Order of values$",
      "title": "05.01 — Resolve tradeoffs in product order",
      "description": "A change begins with priorities, not code. User data and approval boundaries come first, followed by the vital outcome, truthful desktop/mobile support, whole-system comprehension, direct Obsidian APIs, minimal machinery, and only then optimization. This order explains why convenience sometimes loses to a narrower capability."
    },
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## The Pareto rule$",
      "title": "05.02 — Choose the smallest complete outcome",
      "description": "Bolovan prefers one excellent common path over a menu of speculative variants. New concepts must earn their maintenance cost, and a feature is complete only when its important end-to-end behavior works. Smallness is a product capability because one maintainer must be able to reason about the whole plugin."
    },
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## Obsidian first$",
      "title": "05.03 — Ask Obsidian before building infrastructure",
      "description": "Before adding a package, wrapper, index, storage system, or lifecycle mechanism, look for the public Obsidian API that already owns the capability. Keep meaningful decision logic testable, but add a boundary only when it isolates real policy rather than ceremony."
    },
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## Product constraints$",
      "title": "05.04 — Preserve the non-negotiable boundaries",
      "description": "The product constraints gather the contracts seen in earlier chapters: mobile-safe runtime, one remote endpoint, single-flight Runs, bounded Obsidian-native tools, exact approval with stale checks, visible portable Brain data, device-owned branches, immediate Stop semantics, and sealed plugin writes."
    },
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## Comprehension budget$",
      "title": "05.05 — Keep the common path in one head",
      "description": "A routine action should cross no more than four production modules, and a routine feature should usually change one policy-owning module plus tests. Crossing the budget is an alarm to simplify, not a reason to split code into shallow files."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/adr/0001-native-cross-platform-harness.md",
      "pattern": "^## Consequences$",
      "title": "05.06 — Use ADRs for load-bearing intent",
      "description": "ADR 0001 records consequences that source code alone cannot explain: one bundle on every target platform, completed-response rendering as the portable guarantee, and excluded capabilities such as shell access. Read an ADR to understand why a seam exists; read current code and tests to understand what it does today."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/adr/0002-read-only-self-inspection.md",
      "pattern": "^## Decision$",
      "title": "05.07 — Record narrow exceptions explicitly",
      "description": "Self-inspection is a useful exception to the hidden-directory boundary, so ADR 0002 names its exact reach: read and list Bolovan's own plugin directory through `Vault.adapter`, while every configuration write remains refused. Exceptions this important should be easy to find and hard to broaden accidentally."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/fake-app.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function fakeApp",
      "title": "05.08 — Exercise policy through a synthetic Obsidian app",
      "description": "`fakeApp` supplies an in-memory Vault, hidden-directory adapter, file manager, and minimal metadata behavior. Module-interface tests can therefore exercise real Bolovan policy without a personal vault, live credentials, provider account, or undocumented files outside the repository."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/model-adapter.test.ts",
      "pattern": "^describe\\(\"OpenAI-compatible model adapter\"",
      "title": "05.09 — Test seams by their observable contract",
      "description": "The adapter suite asserts requests, authorization, optional thinking effort, normalized replies, tool calls, usage, model lists, and provider failures without depending on private helpers. The same interface-first style covers VaultTools, BrainStore, Agent Skills, Transcript, workspace, context, and the Harness."
    },
    {
      "file": "AGENTS.md",
      "pattern": "^## Verification$",
      "title": "05.10 — Match proof to the risk",
      "description": "The verification section names the dangerous contracts that deserve direct coverage: provider normalization, stopping, cancellation, approvals, stale rejection, branch forking, bounded reads, structured files, scans, navigation, and native lifecycle operations. Tests should survive internal refactors while these interfaces remain true."
    },
    {
      "file": "package.json",
      "pattern": "\"build\": \"tsc --noEmit",
      "title": "05.11 — Build the authored runtime",
      "description": "`npm run build` first asks TypeScript to check without emitting, then runs the production esbuild configuration. This catches type and bundle failures before the test runner evaluates behavior."
    },
    {
      "file": "esbuild.config.mjs",
      "pattern": "^const context = await esbuild.context",
      "title": "05.12 — Produce one browser-targeted plugin bundle",
      "description": "esbuild starts at `src/main.ts`, emits `main.js`, targets the browser, and leaves Obsidian external. The runtime source must therefore honor the earlier promise: no Node built-ins, native subprocesses, or desktop-only implementation path."
    },
    {
      "file": "package.json",
      "pattern": "\"test\": \"npm run build",
      "title": "05.13 — Run the complete proof chain",
      "description": "`npm test` composes the build, all Vitest suites, and the built-plugin smoke test. The suite is intentionally standalone: repository fixtures and synthetic adapters replace the surrounding vault and external services."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/load-built-plugin.cjs",
      "pattern": "require\\(temporaryBundle\\)",
      "title": "05.14 — Load what Obsidian will actually load",
      "description": "The final smoke test copies the generated bundle, supplies a lightweight `obsidian` module stub, and requires the bundle. It catches top-level imports and runtime assumptions that source-only tests can miss."
    },
    {
      "file": ".agents/skills/codetour-truth/SKILL.md",
      "pattern": "^# Truth pass$",
      "title": "05.15 — Keep the story true",
      "description": "The final responsibility is this CodeTour itself. The `codetour-truth` skill identifies steps affected by code or behavior changes, re-derives every factual clause from current evidence, preserves numbered story order, validates every anchor, and requires behavior-specific verification. The tour is complete only while it remains a verified map of the repository you just read."
    }
  ]
}
